      "content": "The core legal argument for expanded executive privilege rests on the need for the President to conduct sensitive national security and diplomatic functions without fear of public scrutiny or political interference. This privilege is framed as essential to the President's inherent constitutional duties, allowing them to make high-stakes decisions in the shadows necessary for effective governance. Without this protection, the President would be constantly hampered by legislative demands, rendering the office incapable of executing its duties effectively.",
      "source": "target",
