      "content": "okay, i see the point about national security. but if we look at the actual legal precedents, the expansion of this privilege often seems to be less about \"protecting the office\" and more about just avoiding accountability for questionable decisions. doesn't that make the \"checks and balances\" argument much more objectively grounded in law than the \"necessity\" argument? it seems like the necessity argument is just a convenient excuse for avoiding scrutiny.",
